On 2007-11-07, Tina Hall wrote:
> If I may add a few question concering things to do with objects  
> (possibly spoily, but is there anyone left here who isn't spoiled,  
> except my Swiss cheese memory?):
>
> Does the buc status of tins have any effect on it telling me what's in  
> it? (I only remember I shouldn't eat cockatrice tins, but if I don't  
> know what's in it... I also vaguely remember something about a message  
> of "This smells of". Do I get that every time? Is there some chance of  
> the wrong or a too vague smell?

You get that every time, it'll always be correct (except in the case of
hallucination). Cockatrice meat is something to watch, but also meat
that'll make you a cannibal. BUC doesn't play into the smell message, but
there are other bad effects that are more likely to occur in cursed tins.

> Engraving; what takes just one turn for a whole word? (Some wands, I  
> think, and Magicbane? I vaguely remember digging and fire. Something  
> else?) 

Lightning. And it's not just Magicbane, it's any (non-cursed) athame. And
magic markers. And your fingers. 

>        Engraving in the floor can be erased with time, no? Fire stays,  
> right? (What else?)

The persistence of the engraving goes burned (fire/lightning) > engraved
(athame/digging) > written in dust (fingers). Burned is permanent.

> Holy water: I've got a blessed clear potion, an uncursed clear potion,  
> and three cursed clear potions (from one of my dead Priests, or rather  
> his bones). What means are there to clear those (and whatever potions I  
> may want to clear - so far I've only got a bunch of unidentified ones).  

Er? They are clear. If you want to change their BUC, the same methods as
for anything else apply. Non-clear potions can be cleared by dipping into
fountains (side effects), dipping into pools (them or yourself [side
effects]), and wands of cancellation (for most potion types).

> I've read the spoiler on dipping in fountains (I don't think I want to  
> do that). 

You can convert a fountain into a pool with a pickaxe. Then you can dip
your potions with boots of water walking on, or jump in with the potions
after dropping anything you don't want water damaged.

